WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.179 --> 00:00:13.980
In a world that is facing unprecedented increases
in population and with larger and larger cities,

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.969
you could wonder... do cities have limits?

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:16.969
Well...

00:00:16.969 --> 00:00:17.969
The truth, folks, is yes.

00:00:17.969 --> 00:00:23.260
That’s why in some countries, they’re
opting for a much more drastic solution than

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:24.260
usual.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.410
I’m referring to the so-called “cities
from scratch”.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.050
But, what exactly are cities from scratch?

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:34.980
Well..., with “cities from scratch” we’re
talking about cities created, constructed

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:40.460
and populated where there used to be absolutely
nothing more than a vast expanse...of land

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:41.460
or sea.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.630
Take for example Songdo, in South Korea.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:48.780
Songdo, which was also turned into a Special
Economic Zone, is one of the world's greatest

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.100
examples of what a city built from scratch
should be.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.460
A true example of success.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:01:00.300
Because, some of the others didn’t do so
well, and some were a complete failure.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:01.980
But let’s pause for a second.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:07.399
Some of you may be thinking that this is crazy,
that it’s too expensive to build a city

00:01:07.399 --> 00:01:10.480
from scratch, right?

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:17.540
Well, folks… you’re absolutely right.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:22.990
China, for example, has used more concrete
in the last three years than the United States

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:24.900
did throughout the 20th century.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.290
But, why are so many new cities being built
right now?

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:32.530
Is it just about offering more housing to
the people coming from the countryside?

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.369
Or are there more reasons behind it?

00:01:35.369 --> 00:01:40.900
Let's take a closer look.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:47.520
(THE POWER OF CITIES)

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.450
Folks, it's a fact that power is concentrated
in cities.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:56.310
And I’m not only talking about political
power, I’m also talking about economic power,

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:59.259
industrial power, artistic power, cultural
power...

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:05.420
Cities are something of a cocktail of elements
that lead to a prosperity explosion.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:13.790
And it’s precisely beneath all that prosperity
that we find the reasons to create new cities,

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:14.790
cities from scratch.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:18.299
But, you may be wondering, where the hell
are all these new cities being built?

00:02:18.299 --> 00:02:21.870
Well... let's see, in Europe I can think of
very few examples that fit.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:26.030
In America there are some but they are on
a smaller scale...

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:27.030
So...

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.730
Where can they be?

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.480
Any ideas?

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.129
Exactly, you got it!

00:02:32.129 --> 00:02:35.239
Asia is the greatest proponent of new cities
in the world.

00:02:35.239 --> 00:02:43.180
And within Asia there’s one country that
has taken it to the extreme.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:48.069
China has built more than 600 cities from
scratch since the Chinese Communist Party

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:49.500
came to power in 1949.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.719
And not all of these cities were built with
the same function in mind.

00:02:53.719 --> 00:02:58.019
For example, some are centers of state or
regional political power, others are oriented

00:02:58.019 --> 00:03:02.799
towards logistics, others are technological,
many others seek to be trade and finance centers,

00:03:02.799 --> 00:03:06.389
and of course some are chiefly residential.

00:03:06.389 --> 00:03:11.489
However, the bottom line is that the purpose
of the city doesn’t really matter, they’re

00:03:11.489 --> 00:03:17.019
all built with a common ambition: to be engines
of long-term economic growth.

00:03:17.019 --> 00:03:24.349
China plans to build new city nearly three
times the size of New York.

00:03:24.349 --> 00:03:26.639
The Guardian.)

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:34.720
Yes, we’re talking about an extreme case
of urban planning... but market-targeted planning.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.389
Which doesn’t necessarily mean it’ll go
well… but we’ll see more of that a bit

00:03:39.389 --> 00:03:40.389
later.

00:03:40.389 --> 00:03:42.989
The point, folks, is that the relationship
between cities and economic development isn’t

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:47.099
only being pursued by China, but also by other
developing countries that are experiencing

00:03:47.099 --> 00:03:50.430
a fiercel transfer of people from the countryside
to the city.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.430
Oh!

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:54.129
And I can already tell you that this is something
we’ll be talking about in the future on

00:03:54.129 --> 00:03:55.129
VisualPolitik.

00:03:55.129 --> 00:03:58.669
Because it isn’t only happening in Asia.

00:03:58.669 --> 00:04:03.730
In Africa, there are projects such as Eko-Atlantic
in Nigeria, Hope City in Ghana, Konza in Kenya

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:10.689
or the New Administrative Capital – yes,
that’s its name – in Egypt.

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:15.489
Of course, in order for this to be a success,
we need to know how to build a city from scratch,

00:04:15.489 --> 00:04:20.419
and for that we must take many important things
into account such as location, water supply,

00:04:20.419 --> 00:04:24.590
capital investment, public transport and waste
management among many other things.

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:32.120
And let’s not forget to create a city identity
within which people feel comfortable and integrated.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.539
As you can imagine, all this is very expensive,
that’s why not all the new cities have worked

00:04:36.539 --> 00:04:37.539
as planned.

00:04:37.539 --> 00:04:42.270
Even considering all the risk, new city fever
is very common in several developing countries.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:44.650
So, before we go on, we need to consider something:

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.600
Do all cities follow the same model?

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.870
Are they all driven in the same way?

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:55.220
Listen closely to the answer because it may
surprise you.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:05:02.720
(THE PARADIGM OF PUBLIC AND PRIVATE CITIES)

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:04.830
Yes folks, you heard that right.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:05.830
Private cities

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:09.599
As with everything, there isn’t just one
way of doing things, and therefore there isn’t

00:05:09.599 --> 00:05:11.490
a single city management model.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:15.939
Okay, it’s true, most new cities are driven
and managed by the public sector, that is,

00:05:15.939 --> 00:05:20.659
by the State, by governments ... But, folks,
a small number of them have been created and

00:05:20.659 --> 00:05:22.500
managed by the private sector.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.430
And some more successfully than others.

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:27.710
But... let me ask you a question, what do
you think are the main differences between

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:32.870
the two models?

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:37.370
When we talk about private cities, you may
imagine a huge wall with security guards at

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:42.979
the doors controlling who can or cannot pass,
or charging a fee for entry.

00:05:42.979 --> 00:05:44.340
Well...

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.150
Nothing could be further from the truth.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:51.949
Private cities are ordinary cities whose only
peculiarity is that they have been designed,

00:05:51.949 --> 00:05:55.290
built and later managed by one or more private
companies.

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:59.330
That is, instead of receiving services from
the public administration through the municipality

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:03.900
and taxes, these services are provided by
private companies through a kind of general

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:07.740
service contract that the residents sign when
they move in and that is paid annually.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:13.680
Perhaps one of the best examples of this is
Reston.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:20.509
See, Reston is a private city in Virginia,
in the United States.

00:06:20.509 --> 00:06:22.539
It belongs to Fairfax County.

00:06:22.539 --> 00:06:28.569
Founded in the early 1960s, it was conceived
as a set of company-created owner communities.

00:06:28.569 --> 00:06:33.210
And it more or less continues to be so to
this day.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:37.639
These communities have their own private security
service, garbage collection and public transport.

00:06:37.639 --> 00:06:39.900
And it looks like things have gone quite well…

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.840
The city has had a huge population growth.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:46.879
Between 1970 and 1980 alone, the population
skyrocketed from less than 6,000 to more than

00:06:46.879 --> 00:06:53.830
36,000, a 536% increase in ten years.

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.069
Today more than 60,000 people live in Reston.

00:06:56.069 --> 00:07:00.849
And it makes sense, Reston, for example, has
been cataloged by a CNN study as one of the

00:07:00.849 --> 00:07:04.509
best small cities in the United States to
live in.

00:07:04.509 --> 00:07:09.310
And that isn’t the only example…

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:15.590
Lavasa, in India, is a private city in development
that will have an area of ​​100 square

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:18.780
kilometers and 300,000 inhabitants when completed.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:21.830
Today it’s one of the best places to live
in India thanks to its services.

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:25.680
In any case, we’ll need to see if this way
of making cities takes off in the upcoming

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:26.680
years.

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:35.389
Because, the truth is, most cities don’t
follow this private model.

00:07:35.389 --> 00:07:36.919
And folks, let's not kid ourselves.

00:07:36.919 --> 00:07:41.030
A new city being public or private doesn’t
define its success or failure.

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:45.249
Several factors come into play to make cities
as we want them to be: true hubs of growth

00:07:45.249 --> 00:07:46.400
and prosperity.

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:50.420
And among them the most important one is urban
planning, with a vision of the future, but

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:53.520
also remaining realistic and thinking about
the people.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:58.960
Well, there is a country where these basic
concepts have gotten quite out of hand.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:02.580
Listen up.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:08.990
(THE DANGERS OF BECOMING AN ADDICT)

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:12.530
Cities, cities and more cities...

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:20.400
We told you at the beginning, China has a
clear idea in mind: the future is in the cities.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:23.960
But, to what extent?

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:27.870
When should a country say STOP, we don’t
need more new cities?

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:32.690
Well... folks, the Chinese government doesn’t
seem to want to see what the rest of the world

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:36.669
sees: that they have a problem, they’re
going to need a lot of people to inhabit their

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:39.250
mammoth cities built from scratch.

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:44.670
China’s shrinking cities are still addicted
to building despite population slump.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:45.670
South China Morning Post.)

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:49.810
And, of course, building a city with everyone
else's money leads to a huge illusion of economic

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:50.810
prosperity.

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:54.470
A lot of players become involved, and whole
economic sectors continue to work thanks to

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:55.930
the new city’s construction.

00:08:55.930 --> 00:09:00.470
For example, Chinese local governments have
covered a lot of their budget for years thanks

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:04.710
to real estate developments in these new cities.

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:13.750
You see, in the year 2000, land sales accounted
for 9.3% of municipal revenues in China.

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:18.690
Well... listen to this, in 2011, after more
than 10 years of new city booms, this amount

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:23.270
reached a whopping 74.1% of total revenues.

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:29.550
Basically, they thought they’d found the
goose that laid the golden eggs.

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:34.620
According to a joint survey done by Landesa,
Renmin University and Michigan State University,

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:39.470
Chinese municipalities earned 40 times more
money selling land for urban development than

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:41.420
they had paid to expropriate them.

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:46.220
And in the case of land reclaimed from the
sea, this ratio could increase up to 100 times

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:47.220
more.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:49.940
With these numbers, it doesn’t seem like
bad business, right?

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:54.670
However, folks,hold on a second before we
start ringing the bells.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:59.120
On the one hand local governments spent all
that money and much more building infrastructure

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:00.420
and projects of all kinds.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:05.620
After all, it seemed like the money was always
going to fall from the sky and when the apartments

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:14.170
were full of people... they’d get more and
more money.

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:18.560
There was such euphoria that several local
governments even ended up becoming developers.

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:20.980
Which led them to accumulate a huge amount
of debt.

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:25.970
But, of course, this model is enormously short-sighted
and is quickly losing ground in China.

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:30.010
Come on, if you extend the process too much...
you’ll end up with dozens of completely

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:34.400
empty cities full of abandoned or half-built
residential blocks because nobody is living

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:35.400
in them.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:36.680
And forget about new taxes and all that.

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:37.779
Of course, the debt won’t disappear.

00:10:37.779 --> 00:10:40.010
Well, that’s exactly what is happening.

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:48.690
As you can see in the graph, at the national
level, the square kilometers built have already

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:52.560
surpassed the units of 10,000 people living
in urban areas.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:56.440
And 10,000 inhabitants per square kilometer
is considered a good measure of population

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:58.560
density for the new Chinese cities.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:03.740
Basically, this means that the rhythm of construction
is already much quicker than the urban population

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:08.399
growth required to occupy those cities.

00:11:08.399 --> 00:11:12.350
That is, the perfect recipe for building empty
cities... or as they’re known in China,

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:14.000
ghost cities.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:17.240
China local governments' hidden debt could
total $5.8 trillion: S&amp;P.

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:18.430
Reuters) October, 16, 2018

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:22.389
Folks, we could say that the local Chinese
governments have been affected by something

00:11:22.389 --> 00:11:25.670
like the Dutch disease, the curse of natural
resources.

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:30.430
Although in this case, instead of natural
resources, we have real estate promotions

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:33.260
financed and promoted by China’s own public
banks.

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:39.839
Even so, Chinese President Xi Jinping has
a very, very different opinion.

00:11:39.839 --> 00:11:44.310
President Xi himself has promoted the construction
of another new city from scratch.His personal

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:48.709
project, the New Area of ​​Xiongan, is
an area 120 kilometers from Beijing that is

00:11:48.709 --> 00:11:52.350
connected by a 20-minute trip from the Chinese
capital by high-speed train.

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:55.600
Xi wants it to become a hub for the technology
industry.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:59.680
We’re talking about a completely ecological
city for which so many trees are going to

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:04.519
be planted that the forest cover in the area
will expand from 11 to 40%.

00:12:04.519 --> 00:12:09.899
With all this, folks, we can assure you of
something: China plans to continue building

00:12:09.899 --> 00:12:13.820
new cities despite having many empty or clearly
underpopulated ones.

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:15.920
So we must ask ourselves...

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:17.889
Will the Chinese new city bubble definitely
explode?

00:12:17.889 --> 00:12:21.430
And if so, how will this affect their economy?

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:26.500
We’ve already told you that one of China’s
features is its enormous level of savings,

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:29.170
which may, indeed, finance all these...

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:30.170
Wasteful expenses?

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:31.170
But...

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:35.660
Is it really reasonable to waste soooo much
money on cities that are often ending up empty?

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:39.339
Will real estate developers and banks go bankrupt?

00:12:39.339 --> 00:12:45.449
What could happen to the global economy if
this true addiction to building new cities

00:12:45.449 --> 00:12:47.589
expanded to other developing countries?

00:12:47.589 --> 00:12:49.550
Is it bread today and hunger tomorrow?

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:51.269
Leave your answer in the comments.

